                                <div class="main-content" >
                                    <section data-title="Background"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec1">Background</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec1-content"><p>Adrenarche is a major developmental milestone that marks the onset of the juvenile phase of human development [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Del Giudice M, Barrett ES, Belsky J, Hartman S, Martel MM, Sangenstedt S, et al. Individual differences in developmental plasticity: a role for early androgens? Psychoneuroendocrinology. 2018;90:165–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.psyneuen.2018.02.025&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e933">1</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Hochberg Z. Juvenility in the context of life history theory. Arch Dis Child. 2008;93:534–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1136/ADC.2008.137570&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e936">2</a>], during which the adrenal gland <i>zona reticularis</i> emerges, with high 17,20-lyase activity of CYP17A1 and low 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (3βHSD) activity, leading to the production of dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and its sulfated form, DHEAS (together referred to as DHEA(S) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Auchus RJ, Rainey WE. Adrenarche - physiology, biochemistry and human disease. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2004;60:288–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1046/J.1365-2265.2003.01858.X&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e942">3</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Nakamura Y, Gang HX, Suzuki T, Sasano H, Rainey WE. Adrenal changes associated with adrenarche. Rev Endocr Metab Disord. 2009;10:19–26. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11154-008-9092-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e945">4</a>]. Circulating levels of DHEA(S) become detectable in children at around 6 years of age and peak in early adulthood, during which DHEAS is the most abundant steroid hormone in the circulation [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Auchus RJ, Rainey WE. Adrenarche - physiology, biochemistry and human disease. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2004;60:288–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1046/J.1365-2265.2003.01858.X&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e949">3</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Kim JH, Lee YA, Lim YH, Lee K, Kim BN, Kim JI, et al. Changes in adrenal androgens and steroidogenic enzyme activities from ages 2, 4, to 6 years: a prospective cohort study. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2020;105:3265–72. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/CLINEM/DGAA498&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e952">5</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Liimatta J, Jääskeläinen J, Karvonen AM, Remes S, Voutilainen R, Pekkanen J. Tracking of serum DHEAS concentrations from age 1 to 6 years: a prospective cohort study. J Endocr Soc. 2020;4. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JENDSO/BVAA012&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e952_1">6</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Palmert MR, Hayden DL, Mansfield MJ, Crigler JF, Crowley WF, Chandler DW, et al. The longitudinal study of adrenal maturation during gonadal suppression: evidence that adrenarche is a gradual process. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2001;86:4536–42. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM.86.9.7863&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e955">7</a>]. Yet, remarkably little is understood about the driving factors and physiological effects of these adrenal androgens during childhood, largely due to the lack of an appropriate animal model [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Iñiguez G, Barnafi E, Lalli E, Pereira A, Ferrer P, Mericq V. Prolactin and adrenal androgens during adrenarche. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2025;103:66–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/CEN.15227&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e958">8</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Rege J, Garber S, Conley AJ, Elsey RM, Turcu AF, Auchus RJ, et al. Circulating 11-oxygenated androgens across species. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;190:242–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2019.04.005&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e961">9</a>].</p><p>Adrenarche occurs independently of hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal (HPG) axis activation, typically preceding puberty by 2–3 years. While apparently not essential for sexual maturation, as seen in patients with adrenal insufficiencies [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Counts DR, Pescovitz OH, Barnes KM, Hench KD, Chrousos GP, Sherins RJ, et al. Dissociation of adrenarche and gonadarche in precocious puberty and in isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 1987;64:1174–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM-64-6-1174&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e967">10</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ibáñez L, DiMartino-Nardi J, Potau N, Saenger P. Premature adrenarche--normal variant or forerunner of adult disease? Endocr Rev. 2000;21:671–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/EDRV.21.6.0416&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR11" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e967_1">11</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Sklar CA, Kaplan SL, Grumbacht MM. Evidence for dissociation between adrenarche and gonadarche: studies in patients with idiopathic precocious puberty, gonadal dysgenesis, isolated gonadotropin deficiency, and constitutionally delayed growth and adolescence. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 1980;51:548–56. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM-51-3-548&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR12" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e967_2">12</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Taha D, Mullis PE, Ibáñez L, De Zegher F. Absent or delayed adrenarche in Pit-1/POU1F1 deficiency. Horm Res. 2005;64:175–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1159/000088793&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR13" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e967_3">13</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Sizonenko PC, Paunier L. Hormonal changes in puberty III: correlation of plasma dehydroepiandrosterone, testosterone, FSH, and LH with stages of puberty and bone age in normal boys and girls and in patients with Addison’s disease or hypogonadism or with premature or late adrenarche. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 1975;41:894–904. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM-41-5-894&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e970">14</a>], premature adrenarche is associated with accelerated pubertal events [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ibáñez L, Jiménez R, De Zegher F. Early puberty-menarche after precocious pubarche: relation to prenatal growth. Pediatrics. 2006;117:117–21. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1542/PEDS.2005-0664&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973">15</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Liimatta J, Utriainen P, Voutilainen R, Jääskeläinen J. Girls with a history of premature adrenarche have advanced growth and pubertal development at the age of 12 years. Front Endocrinol (Lausanne). 2017. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3389/FENDO.2017.00291&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_1">16</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Livadas S, Bothou C, Kanaka-Gantenbein C, Chiotis D, Angelopoulos N, Macut D, et al. Unfavorable hormonal and psychologic profile in adult women with a history of premature adrenarche and pubarche, compared to women with polycystic ovary syndrome. Horm Metab Res. 2020;52:179–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1055/A-1109-2630&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_2">17</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Merino PM, Pereira A, Iñiguez G, Corvalan C, Mericq V. High DHEAS level in girls is associated with earlier pubertal maturation and mild increase in androgens throughout puberty without affecting postmenarche ovarian morphology. Horm Res Paediatr. 2019;92:357–64. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1159/000506632&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_3">18</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Rosenfield RL. Normal and premature adrenarche. Endocr Rev. 2021;42:783–814. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/endrev/bnab009&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_4">19</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Thankamony A, Ong KK, Ahmed ML, Ness AR, Holly JMP, Dunger DB. Higher levels of IGF-I and adrenal androgens at age 8 years are associated with earlier age at menarche in girls. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2012;97. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JC.2011-3261&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR20" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_5">20</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Sudman M, Stöger R, Bentley GR, Melamed P. Association of childhood DHEAS concentration, pubertal development and DNA methylation at puberty-related genes. Eur J Endocrinol. 2024. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/EJENDO/LVAE156&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR21" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_6">21</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Pereira A, Iñiguez G, Corvalan C, Mericq V. High DHEAS is associated with earlier pubertal events in girls but not in boys. J Endocr Soc. 2017;1:800–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JS.2017-00120&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR22" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e973_7">22</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 23" title="Santos-Silva R, Fontoura M, Severo M, Santos AC. Dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate levels at 7 years old are positively associated with more advanced pubertal development between 10 and 13 years old in girls. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2022;97:747–54. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/CEN.14805&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR23" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e976">23</a>]. It is also linked to an increased risk for a woman to develop polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Livadas S, Bothou C, Kanaka-Gantenbein C, Chiotis D, Angelopoulos N, Macut D, et al. Unfavorable hormonal and psychologic profile in adult women with a history of premature adrenarche and pubarche, compared to women with polycystic ovary syndrome. Horm Metab Res. 2020;52:179–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1055/A-1109-2630&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e979">17</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 24" title="Ibañez L, Potau N, Virdis R, Zampolli M, Terzi C, Gussinyé M, et al. Postpubertal outcome in girls diagnosed of premature pubarche during childhood: increased frequency of functional ovarian hyperandrogenism. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 1993;76:1599–603. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM.76.6.8501168&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e983">24</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 25" title="Ibáñez L, Díaz R, López-Bermejo A, Marcos MV. Clinical spectrum of premature pubarche: links to metabolic syndrome and ovarian hyperandrogenism. Rev Endocr Metab Disord. 2009;10:63–76. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11154-008-9096-Y&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR25" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e986">25</a>], which is defined by the presence of at least two of the following (after exclusion of other endocrine disorders): hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction and/or polycystic ovarian morphology. Supporting a causal link, postnatal DHEA administration in animal models has been shown to induce PCOS-phenotypes [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 26" title="Stener-Victorin E, Padmanabhan V, Walters KA, Campbell RE, Benrick A, Giacobini P, et al. Animal models to understand the etiology and pathophysiology of polycystic ovary syndrome. Endocr Rev. 2020;41. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ENDREV/BNAA010&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e989">26</a>]. Thus, although there is a connection between the prepubertal rise in DHEA(S) and the regulation of reproductive maturation and later function, the underlying mechanisms are not clear, given that most commonly used laboratory rodents do not undergo adrenarche [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Nakamura Y, Gang HX, Suzuki T, Sasano H, Rainey WE. Adrenal changes associated with adrenarche. Rev Endocr Metab Disord. 2009;10:19–26. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11154-008-9092-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e992">4</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Rege J, Garber S, Conley AJ, Elsey RM, Turcu AF, Auchus RJ, et al. Circulating 11-oxygenated androgens across species. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;190:242–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2019.04.005&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e995">9</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Cutler GB, Glenn M, Bush M, Hodgen GD, Graham CE, Loriaux DL. Adrenarche: a survey of rodents, domestic animals, and primates. Endocrinology. 1978;103:2112–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ENDO-103-6-2112&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR27" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e998">27</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Keeney DS, Jenkins CM, Waterman MR. Developmentally regulated expression of adrenal 17 alpha-hydroxylase cytochrome P450 in the mouse embryo. Endocrinology. 1995;136:4872–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ENDO.136.11.7588219&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR28" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e998_1">28</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 29" title="Le Goascogne C, Sananes N, Gouezou M, Takemori S, Kominami S, Baulieu EE, et al. Immunoreactive cytochrome P-450(17 alpha) in rat and guinea-pig gonads, adrenal glands and brain. J Reprod Fertil. 1991;93:609–22. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JRF.0.0930609&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR29" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1002">29</a>] and transition rapidly from weaning to sexual maturity without a juvenile phase.
</p><p>Another major challenge in elucidating the role of DHEA(S) is that it lacks its own receptor, and its modes of action prior to puberty may differ significantly from those in sexually mature animals. DHEA can be metabolized into more potent sex steroids such as estradiol (E2) and testosterone (T) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Clark BJ, Prough RA, Klinge CM. Mechanisms of action of dehydroepiandrosterone. In: Vitamins and hormones. Vitam Horm; 2018. p. 29–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/bs.vh.2018.02.003&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1008">30</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Prough RA, Clark BJ, Klinge CM. Novel mechanisms for DHEA action. J Mol Endocrinol. 2016;56:R139–55. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JME-16-0013&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR31" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1008_1">31</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 32" title="Remer T, Boye KR, Hartmann MF, Wudy SA. Urinary markers of adrenarche: reference values in healthy subjects, aged 3-18 years. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2005;90:2015–21. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JC.2004-1571&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR32" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1011">32</a>], though the expression and activity of the relevant steroidogenic enzymes during the prepubertal period remain unclear. DHEA might also act directly on steroid hormone receptors. While its affinity for the androgen receptor (AR) and estrogen receptors is much lower than that of cognate ligands [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 30" title="Clark BJ, Prough RA, Klinge CM. Mechanisms of action of dehydroepiandrosterone. In: Vitamins and hormones. Vitam Horm; 2018. p. 29–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/bs.vh.2018.02.003&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1014">30</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 31" title="Prough RA, Clark BJ, Klinge CM. Novel mechanisms for DHEA action. J Mol Endocrinol. 2016;56:R139–55. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JME-16-0013&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR31" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1017">31</a>], its very high circulating concentrations, combined with the absence of competing gonadal steroids at significant levels before puberty, could allow receptor activation during this developmental window.</p><p>Although adrenarche has been thought to occur only in humans and a few primates, recent studies reported that the spiny mouse has not only an extended juvenile period reminiscent of that during human development, but also prepubertal adrenal androgen synthesis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="Greaves RF, Wudy SA, Badoer E, Zacharin M, Hirst JJ, Quinn T, et al. A tale of two steroids: the importance of the androgens DHEA and DHEAS for early neurodevelopment. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;188:77–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2018.12.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1023">33</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 34" title="Quinn TA, Ratnayake U, Dickinson H, Nguyen TH, McIntosh M, Castillo-Melendez M, et al. Ontogeny of the adrenal gland in the spiny mouse, with particular reference to production of the steroids cortisol and dehydroepiandrosterone. Endocrinology. 2013;154:1190–201. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/en.2012-1953&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR34" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1026">34</a>]. This precocial rodent is born with open eyes and ears, as well as a relatively mature brain [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 35" title="Merkulyeva N. Comparative review of the brain development in Acomys cahirinus. Neurosci Biobehav Rev. 2024;167. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.NEUBIOREV.2024.105939&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR35" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1029">35</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 36" title="Zeiss CJ. Comparative milestones in rodent and human postnatal central nervous system development. Toxicol Pathol. 2021;49:1368–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1177/01926233211046933&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1032">36</a>]. It weans around postnatal day (PND) 14–21, but does not enter puberty until approximately PND 45, with circulating DHEA levels peaking around PND 20 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="Greaves RF, Wudy SA, Badoer E, Zacharin M, Hirst JJ, Quinn T, et al. A tale of two steroids: the importance of the androgens DHEA and DHEAS for early neurodevelopment. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;188:77–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2018.12.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1035">33</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 37" title="Peitz B. The oestrous cycle of the spiny mouse (Acomys cahirinus). J Reprod Fertil. 1981;61:453–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JRF.0.0610453&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR37" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1039">37</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 38" title="Haughton CL, Gawriluk TR, Seifert AW. The biology and husbandry of the African spiny mouse (Acomys cahirinus) and the research uses of a laboratory colony. J Am Assoc Lab Anim Sci. 2016;55:9–17." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR38" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1042">38</a>]. This extended juvenile period, combined with endogenous prepubertal DHEA production, positions the spiny mouse as a useful model for investigating the developmental roles of DHEA during adrenarche. Here we used this novel animal model to investigate how a rise in prepubertal DHEA affects reproductive tissues, hypothesizing adrenarche contributes to priming HPG axis maturation by modifying gene expression.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Results"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec2-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec2">Results</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec2-content"><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec3">The increase in DHEA levels in prepubertal spiny mice occurs independently of activation of the central control of reproduction</h3><p>Initially we sought to corroborate a previous report that DHEA levels are elevated in the prepubertal spiny mice [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="Greaves RF, Wudy SA, Badoer E, Zacharin M, Hirst JJ, Quinn T, et al. A tale of two steroids: the importance of the androgens DHEA and DHEAS for early neurodevelopment. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;188:77–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2018.12.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1057">33</a>]. Indeed, DHEA serum concentrations at 23–24 days were significantly higher (<i>p</i> value 0.0046 and 0.0004 in females and males, respectively) than at 7–8-days-of-age in both sexes (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig1">1</a>A, B), confirming that this comprises an “adrenarche-like” state 2.5–3 weeks before pubertal onset [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="Greaves RF, Wudy SA, Badoer E, Zacharin M, Hirst JJ, Quinn T, et al. A tale of two steroids: the importance of the androgens DHEA and DHEAS for early neurodevelopment. J Steroid Biochem Mol Biol. 2019;188:77–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsbmb.2018.12.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1066">33</a>]. Although we did not examine DHEA levels in older spiny mice, the previous study which continued up to 160 days, indicates that this age corresponds to their peak circulating concentrations.</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-1" data-title="Fig. 1"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig1" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 1</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig1_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-1-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig1_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 1" loading="lazy" width="685" height="502"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2/figures/1" data-track-dest="link:Figure1 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 1" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-1-desc"><p>Prepubertal DHEA elevation in spiny mice occurs independently of central reproductive axis activation. <b>A</b>,<b> B</b> Box plots showing DHEA concentrations in the two age groups of spiny mice: <b>A</b> females (for 7–8 days, <i>n</i> = 10; for 23–24 days, <i>n</i> = 7) and <b>B</b> males (for 7–8 days, <i>n</i> = 7; for 23–24 days, <i>n</i> = 10). Each dot represents levels in an individual spiny mouse. Student’s <i>t</i> test: * <i>P</i> value 0.01014249, *** <i>P</i> value 0.0008173152. <b>C</b> Volcano plot showing hypothalamic DEGs in female spiny mice, with gene names shown for those with <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05: in red where log2FC &gt; 0 and in blue where log2FC &lt; 0. <b>D</b>–<b>J</b> Normalized counts of <b>D</b> <i>Mkrn3</i>, <b>E</b> <i>Vgf</i>, <b>F</b> <i>Dlk1</i>, <b>G</b> <i>Pdyn</i>, <b>H</b> <i>Kiss1</i>, <b>I</b> <i>Kiss1r</i>, and <b>J</b> <i>Gnrh1</i> mRNA levels in these two age groups of female spiny mice. * <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05, ## <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.01, otherwise <i>P</i> value &gt; 0.05, analyzed in DESeq2. <b>K</b>,<b> L</b> Pituitary mRNA levels of <b>K</b> <i>Lhb</i> and <b>L</b> <i>Fshb</i>, measured by qPCR, shown after normalization and relative to levels in the 7–8-day-old group. See also Fig. S1</p></div></div></figure></div><p>We went on to examine whether this increase in DHEA is associated with changes in gene expression. Long-read sequencing of hypothalamic transcriptomes, performed only in females in which puberty occurs earlier and is easier to detect than in males, revealed 373 up- or 510 downregulated genes (at <i>p</i> value &lt; 0.05). Gene Ontology (GO) analysis was performed on this ranked list, which revealed that the upregulated genes in the 23–24-day-old group were enriched for regulation of ion transport, while the downregulated genes were particularly enriched for genes involved in neuron/axon ensheathment and myelination (Additional file 1: Fig. S1A). These downregulated genes included the myelin basic protein <i>Mbp</i> and <i>Sirt2</i> (Additional file 1: Fig. S1B, C) which also has a role in brain myelination [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 39" title="Beirowski B, Gustin J, Armour SM, Yamamoto H, Viader A, North BJ, et al. Sir-two-homolog 2 (Sirt2) modulates peripheral myelination through polarity protein Par-3/atypical protein kinase C (aPKC) signaling. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2011;108. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1073/PNAS.1104969108&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR39" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1219">39</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 40" title="Ma XR, Zhu X, Xiao Y, Gu HM, Zheng SS, Li L, et al. Restoring nuclear entry of Sirtuin 2 in oligodendrocyte progenitor cells promotes remyelination during ageing. Nat Commun. 2022;13. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/S41467-022-28844-1&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR40" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1222">40</a>]. While we did not pursue this direction, adrenarche is a time of brain plasticity and neuronal pruning [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 41" title="Stiles J, Jernigan TL. The basics of brain development. Neuropsychol Rev. 2010;20:327–48. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11065-010-9148-4&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR41" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1226">41</a>], and these changes might reflect the alterations in white matter microstructure and cortical thickness that are seen in adrenarcheal children [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Barendse MEA, Simmons JG, Byrne ML, Seal ML, Patton G, Mundy L, et al. Brain structural connectivity during adrenarche: associations between hormone levels and white matter microstructure. Psychoneuroendocrinology. 2018;88:70–7. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.PSYNEUEN.2017.11.009&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR42" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1229">42</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Barendse MEA, Simmons JG, Smith RE, Seal ML, Whittle S. Adrenarcheal hormone-related development of white matter during late childhood. Neuroimage. 2020;223. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.NEUROIMAGE.2020.117320&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR43" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1229_1">43</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 44" title="Nguyen TV, McCracken JT, Ducharme S, Cropp BF, Botteron KN, Evans AC, et al. Interactive effects of dehydroepiandrosterone and testosterone on cortical thickness during early brain development. J Neurosci. 2013;33:10840–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1523/JNEUROSCI.5747-12.2013&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR44" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1232">44</a>].</p><p>After adjustment for multiple comparison (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05), 9 of these differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were significantly upregulated and 10 downregulated in the 23–24-day-old group (Additional file 1: Fig. 1C, Additional file 2: Table 1 and Additional file 1: Table 2). These DEGs included genes reported to change leading up to pubertal onset [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Abreu AP, Dauber A, Macedo DB, Noel SD, Brito VN, Gill JC, et al. Central precocious puberty caused by mutations in the imprinted gene MKRN3. N Engl J Med. 2013;368:2467–75. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1302160&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR45" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241">45</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="De Vries L, Gat-Yablonski G, Dror N, Singer A, Phillip M. A novel MKRN3 missense mutation causing familial precocious puberty. Hum Reprod. 2014;29:2838–43. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/humrep/deu256&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR46" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_1">46</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shalev D, Melamed P. The role of the hypothalamus and pituitary epigenomes in central activation of the reproductive axis at puberty. Mol Cell Endocrinol. 2020;518:111031. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mce.2020.111031&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR47" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_2">47</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shalev D, Golan G, Pnueli L, Kahan A, Mandel-Gutfreund Y, Melamed P. High-throughput transcriptome analysis reveals a developmental increase in Acvr1c which mediates epigenetic repression of the gene encoding the pubertal brake, makorin ring finger protein 3. NAR Mol Med. 2025;2:8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/NARMME/UGAF008&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR48" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_3">48</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Macedo DB, Kaiser UB. DLK1, Notch signaling and the timing of puberty. Semin Reprod Med. 2019;37:174–81. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0039-3400963&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR49" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_4">49</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Hahm S, Mizuno TM, Wu TJ, Wisor JP, Priest CA, Kozak CA, et al. Targeted deletion of the Vgf gene indicates that the encoded secretory peptide precursor plays a novel role in the regulation of energy balance. Neuron. 1999;23:537–48. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0896-6273(00)80806-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR50" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_5">50</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Aguilar E, Pineda R, Gaytán F, Sánchez-Garrido MA, Romero M, Romero-Ruiz A, et al. Characterization of the reproductive effects of the Vgf-derived peptide TLQP-21 in female rats: in vivo and in vitro studies. Neuroendocrinology. 2013;98:38–50. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1159/000350323&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR51" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1241_6">51</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 52" title="Pinilla L, Pineda R, Gaytán F, Romero M, García-Galiano D, Sánchez-Garrido MA, et al. Characterization of the reproductive effects of the anorexigenic VGF-derived peptide TLQP-21: in vivo and in vitro studies in male rats. Am J Physiol Endocrinol Metab. 2011;300. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1152/AJPENDO.00598.2010&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR52" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1244">52</a>], such as the drop in expression of <i>Mkrn3</i> and increase in <i>Vgf</i> (the increase in <i>Dlk1</i> was significant only prior to adjustment; Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig1">1</a>D–F). Notably, however, the expression of key genes involved in the central control of reproduction, <i>Pdyn, Kiss1</i>, <i>Kiss1R</i>, and <i>Gnrh1</i> all remained unchanged (<i>P</i> &gt; 0.05: Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig1">1</a>G–J). We assessed also, by qPCR, the levels of the downstream <i>Lhb</i> and <i>Fshb</i> genes in the pituitaries of these spiny mice. Both genes were expressed at similar levels across the age groups (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig1">1</a>K, L), confirming that the high level of circulating DHEA at this time was not linked to activation of the central reproductive axis.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec4">“Adrenarche” in spiny mice is associated with increased expression of ovarian steroidogenic enzymes</h3><p>We next looked to determine whether the spiny mouse “adrenarche” is associated with changes in ovarian gene expression, which we assessed similarly by long-read RNA-seq analysis. This analysis indicated 108 upregulated and 99 downregulated genes (log2FC &gt; 1 or &lt; − 1; <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05) (Additional file 3: Table 3). GO analysis revealed that some of the most enriched upregulated biological processes included steroid biosynthesis and metabolic processes, while the most strongly downregulated genes indicated significant enrichment for biological processes related to development and differentiation (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>A).</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-2" data-title="Fig. 2"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig2" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 2</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig2_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-2-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig2_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 2" loading="lazy" width="685" height="479"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2/figures/2" data-track-dest="link:Figure2 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 2" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-2-desc"><p>“Adrenarche” in spiny mice is associated with increased expression of ovarian steroidogenic enzymes. <b>A</b> Enriched biological processes by “GOrilla” gene ontology for the most upregulated (green, <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 and log2FC &gt; 1) and the most downregulated genes (red, <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 and log2FC &lt; − 1) in the ovaries of the 23–24-day-old group of spiny mice. <b>B</b>–<b>I</b> Normalized counts of <b>B</b> <i>Lhcgr</i>, <b>C</b> <i>Cyp11a1</i>, <b>D</b> <i>Star</i>, <b>E</b> <i>Cyp17a1</i>, <b>F</b> <i>Cyb5a</i>, <b>G</b> <i>Hsd17b1</i>, <b>H</b> <i>Fshr</i>, <b>I</b> <i>Greb1</i>, in ovaries of female spiny mice aged 7–8 days (green) and 23–24 days (blue). * <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05, ** <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.01, *** <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.001, # <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05, analyzed in DESeq2 (<b>J</b>). Scheme of genes involved in steroidogenesis in the ovary. Genes in green were significantly upregulated (those with preceding “#” were significant prior to adjustment)</p></div></div></figure></div><p>Most notably, the mRNA levels of the Lh receptor, <i>Lhcgr,</i> were much higher in the 23–24-day-old group (<i>P</i>. adj = 0.001) (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>B). Numerous steroidogenic enzymes also had higher expression levels, e.g., <i>Cyp11a1</i> (<i>P</i>. adj = 0.046, Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>C) and <i>Star</i> (<i>P</i>. adj = 0.027, Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>D), both of which are activated downstream of Lhcgr in Theca cells and are required for the first step of steroidogenesis. Further downstream in the pathway leading to androgen synthesis, <i>Cyp17a1</i> (<i>P</i> value = 0.035), its cofactor <i>Cyb5a</i> (<i>p</i>. adj = 0.045), and <i>Hsd17b1</i> (<i>P</i>. adj = 0.002) were also all elevated (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>E–G). The Fsh receptor (Fshr) is expressed in granulosa cells, and its mRNA levels were also marginally higher (<i>P</i> value = 0.035) (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>H) in the 23–24-day-old spiny mice. The estrogen-responsive gene <i>Greb1</i> was significantly upregulated (<i>P</i>. adj = 7.22e -06) (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>I), indicating estrogen-receptor-mediated activity. There is thus a clear change in ovarian activity (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>J) in the prepubertal spiny mouse ovaries at the time of elevated DHEA levels, and some indication that they might be at least partially steroidogenic, even before the increase in gonadotropin levels at puberty.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec5">DHEA activates estrogen-responsive Prlr and Prl expression in the prepubertal ovary and pituitary</h3><p>Having identified the hypothalamic and ovarian DEGs associated with the increase in DHEA levels, we looked next to elucidate a possible role for DHEA in their regulation and the underlying mechanisms. To this aim, we sought first to identify transcription factors that might mediate the changes in gene expression, by searching for common binding motifs in the promoters of the most significant DEGs. Most notably, estrogen receptor-related activity was implicated, with highly enriched motifs in the DEGs of both tissues for estrogen-related receptors alpha and gamma (ESRRA, ESRRG), and for ESR2 in the ovary. The binding motif of steroidogenic factor 1 (SF-1/NR5A1), which activates steroidogenic-related gene expression and interacts directly with ESR1 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 53" title="Melamed P, Zhu Y, Siew HT, Xie M, Koh M. Gonadotropin-releasing hormone activation of C-jun, but not early growth response factor-1, stimulates transcription of a luteinizing hormone β-subunit gene. Endocrinology. 2006;147:3598–605. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/EN.2006-0022&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR53" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1479">53</a>], was also prominently enriched among DEGs of both tissues (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>A).</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-3" data-title="Fig. 3"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig3" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 3</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig3_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-3-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig3_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 3" loading="lazy" width="685" height="729"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2/figures/3" data-track-dest="link:Figure3 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 3" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-3-desc"><p>DHEA activates estrogen-responsive <i>Prlr</i> and <i>Prl</i> expression in ovary and pituitary. <b>A</b> Enriched motifs in regulatory regions of hypothalamic and ovarian DEGs, and predicted transcription factors (TFs), analyzed using XSTREME in the MEME suite. <b>B</b> Normalized counts of <i>Prlr</i> in ovaries of spiny mice, presented as in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig2">2</a>; <i>P</i>. adj = 0.0004. <b>C</b>–<b>E</b> KK-1 granulosa cells were treated with DHEA (<i>n</i> = 4–5), DHT (<i>n</i> = 4), E2 (<i>n</i> = 4) for 48 h, or nontreated (NT, <i>n</i> = 8). The mRNA levels of <b>C</b> <i>Prlr</i>, <b>D</b> <i>Lhcgr</i>, and <b>E</b> <i>Greb1</i> were measured by qPCR and normalized levels shown relative to those in NT cells. mean ± SEM, ANOVA (<i>P</i> value shown) followed by a post hoc Tukey HSD test, * <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05, ** <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.01, compared to NT. <b>F</b>–<b>H</b> Both ovaries from individual spiny mice were cultured, some treated with DHEA, and mRNA levels of <b>F</b> <i>Prlr</i>, <b>G</b> <i>Lhcgr</i>, and <b>H</b> <i>Greb1</i> measured by qPCR. Fold change compared to NT cells (depicted by a dashed horizontal line) was calculated for normalized values of each (<i>n</i> = 6 animals) and averaged; ANOVA followed by Tukey HSD test compared all values: ** <i>P</i> &lt; 0.01. <b>I</b> <i>Prl</i> mRNA levels in pituitaries of spiny mice across adrenarche, measured and presented as in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig1">1</a>K, L; * <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 (in DESeq2). <b>J</b> <i>Prl</i> mRNA levels in GH3 lactotrope cells, treated with DHEA or E2 for 48 h, or NT (<i>n</i> = 4), presented as above. Student’s <i>t</i> test compared levels with those in NT cells; ** <i>P</i> &lt; 0.01. <b>K</b> GH3 cells were treated with DHEA (<i>n</i> = 4) or E2 (<i>n</i> = 3), or NT before ChIP with antibody against ERα. Precipitated DNA was measured by qPCR with primers upstream of the <i>Prl</i> gene, labelled relative to the transcriptional start site. Values (mean ± SEM) are IP/input, Student’s <i>t</i> test * <i>P</i>. value &lt; 0.05, ** <i>P.</i> value &lt; 0.01 compared to NT</p></div></div></figure></div><p>Estradiol (E2) regulates transcription of <i>Lhcgr</i>, as well as that of another pituitary hormone receptor, <i>Prlr</i>, which was also elevated very significantly (<i>P</i>. adj = 0.0004, Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>B) in the 23–24-day-old spiny mice. We hypothesized that DHEA might be responsible for these changes, either via binding the ESR directly, or after its conversion to the more potent E2. We thus examined the DHEA effect on these genes in mouse ovarian KK-1 granulosa cells, and exposed them to DHEA, E2, or the non-aromatizable dihydrotestosterone (DHT) at doses reflecting their circulating levels (0.01–10 µM DHEA, or 10 nM for E2 or DHT) [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 54" title="Turcu AF, Rege J, Auchus RJ, Rainey WE. 11-Oxygenated androgens in health and disease. Nat Rev Endocrinol. 2020;16:284–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/S41574-020-0336-X&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR54" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1670">54</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 55" title="Rege J, Rainey WE. The steroid metabolome of adrenarche. J Endocrinol. 2012;214:133–43. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JOE-12-0183&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR55" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1674">55</a>]. The <i>Prlr</i> mRNA levels increased ~ 2.5-fold after exposure to DHEA or E2, but not DHT (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>C). <i>Lhcgr</i> responded similarly, though the response to E2 was greater than to DHEA (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>D). <i>Greb1</i> served as an estrogen-responsive control gene, and also responded only to DHEA and E2 (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>E). We also treated primary ovarian cells from young (17 days) spiny mice with DHEA (0.01–1 µM), which lead to a significant 2.5-fold increase in <i>Prlr</i> and <i>Greb1</i> mRNA at the highest dose (1 µM), while <i>Lhcgr</i> mRNA levels did not change significantly (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>F–H). These results indicate the DHEA can activate expression of <i>Prlr</i>, and possibly <i>Lhcgr</i>.</p><p>Although expression of the pituitary gonadotropin genes was still low in the “adrenarcheal” spiny mice, prolactin (Prl) levels are often seen to increase before puberty [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 56" title="Becú-Villalobos D, Lacau-Mengido IM, Díaz-Torga GS, Libertun C. Ontogenic studies of the neural control of adenohypophyseal hormones in the rat. II. Prolactin. Cell Mol Neurobiol. 1992;12:1–19. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00711635&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR56" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1718">56</a>] and Prl expression is upregulated by E2 [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 57" title="Maurer RA. The journal of biological chemistry estradiol regulates the transcription of the prolactin gene*. J Biol Chem. 1982;257:2133–6. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0021-9258(18)34893-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR57" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1721">57</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 58" title="Shull JD, Gorski J. Estrogen stimulates prolactin gene transcription by a mechanism independent of pituitary protein synthesis. Endocrinology. 1984;114:1150–557. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ENDO-114-5-1550&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1724">58</a>]. We thus measured <i>Prl</i> mRNA levels in the pituitaries of the spiny mice and saw, strikingly, that they were as much as tenfold higher in the 23–24-day-old group (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>I). As this suggested that <i>Prl</i> might also be a target of DHEA-directed activity, we examined the effect of DHEA on <i>Prl</i> expression. Rat GH3 lactotrope cells were exposed to E2 or DHEA, and both treatments led to a significant and similar ~ 2.5-fold increase in <i>Prl</i> mRNA levels (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>J).</p><p>To establish further the direct action of DHEA on the <i>Prl</i> gene, we measured estrogen receptor binding to the locus by chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP). GH3 cells were treated as before and ERα binding quantified along the proximal and distal promoter, at a reported distal enhancer [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 59" title="Lannigan DA, Notides AC. Estrogen receptor selectively binds the “coding strand” of an estrogen responsive element. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 1989;86:863–7. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1073/PNAS.86.3.863&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR59" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e1752">59</a>] and further upstream as a negative control region. The binding of ERα to the distal enhancer was significantly elevated following exposure to E2 or DHEA (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig3">3</a>K), suggesting that DHEA might regulate <i>Prl</i> expression via ERα binding to this site. Given that the pituitary gonadotropins appear still inactive at this stage, it is plausible that a DHEA-induced increase in Prl levels, together with the increase in expression of its receptor in the ovaries might play a role in inducing some of the changes in ovarian transcriptome at this prepubertal stage.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec6">DHEA is produced and partially metabolized by tissues other than the adrenal glands in prepubertal spiny mice</h3><p>In order to determine whether the observed changes in gene expression in the HPG tissues are driven by an increase in adrenal DHEA, as opposed to being merely age-related, we performed adrenalectomy (Adx) 2–3 days after weaning (at 17–18 days), and hoped to restore the DHEA levels via daily administration (AdxDHEA). We then harvested the tissues for gene expression analysis, and measured the circulating steroid levels by mass spectrometry.</p><p>As expected, Adx resulted in a very significant drop in circulating levels of cortisol and corticosterone in both female and male spiny mice, demonstrating also the success of the surgery (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>A, B). The drop in levels of these glucocorticoids was further supported by elevated POMC expression in the pituitary (Additional file 1: Fig. S2A, B), which is consistent with the expected negative-feedback regulation. Most surprisingly, however, DHEA levels did not drop following Adx (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>C), nor did levels of other DHEA-derived steroids (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>D–I) indicating clearly that it is, or can be, produced by other tissue(s).</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-4" data-title="Fig. 4"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig4" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 4</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig4_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-4-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig4_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 4" loading="lazy" width="685" height="403"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2/figures/4" data-track-dest="link:Figure4 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 4" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-4-desc"><p>DHEA is produced and partly metabolized by non-adrenal tissues in prepubertal spiny mice. Levels (area under the curve: AUC) of <b>A</b> cortisol, <b>B</b> corticosterone, <b>C</b> DHEA, <b>D</b> DHEAS, <b>E</b> androstenedione, <b>F</b> 11-ketoandrostenedione, <b>G</b> testosterone, <b>H</b> E2, and <b>I</b> DHT in serum from sham (<i>n</i> = 6, in both sexes), Adx (<i>n</i> = 7 or 5, for females or males, respectively) or AdxDHEA (<i>n</i> = 6 or 5) spiny mice, 7 days after surgery, at age 24 days. Mean ± SEM. ANOVA was performed separately for each sex, followed by a post hoc Tukey HSD test, * <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05, ** <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.01, otherwise not significant. See also Fig. S2</p></div></div></figure></div><p>The DHEA administration led to a similar increase in DHEA levels in both sexes (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>C), without affecting glucocorticoid concentrations (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>A–B). Levels of the sulfonated form, DHEAS, also increased markedly following the DHEA administration, but were more variable in females and did not reach significance in post hoc Tukey HSD analysis (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>D). Still, this suggests that the spiny mice do sulfonate DHEA to DHEAS, and that this can occur outside of the adrenal gland. Both androstenedione (A4) and 11-ketoandrostenedione (11-KA4) were significantly elevated in AdxDHEA mice (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>E–F), while testosterone (T) increased only in females (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>G), and E2 and DHT levels did not change in either sex (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>H–I). These latter findings are in accordance with the low levels of the potent gonadal steroids expected prior to puberty, though it is clear that DHEA can be produced and partially metabolized in extra-adrenal tissues in the prepubertal spiny mice.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec7">DHEA administration resulted in an ovarian transcriptome with features characteristic of PCOS</h3><p>Our initial aim was to establish a causal role for DHEA in regulating gene expression during the prepubertal spiny mice “adrenarche.” However, ultimately, this experiment allowed us to assess the effects of a further increase in DHEA levels at this developmental stage. We examined the levels of selected genes in the hypothalamus and pituitary by qPCR, but these showed very minor, inconsistent or no change (not shown). This included a lack of increase in <i>Prl</i> mRNA, which was surprising given our previous findings in vivo and in vitro, though likely related to the administration regime of high DHEA concentration over a limited interval, rather than a persistent gradual increase. Still, most of the developmental-associated changes in gene expression were in the ovaries, which we therefore assessed by RNA-seq transcriptome analysis.</p><p>Adx appeared to have little effect on the ovary, as seen in the degree of overlap between groups in the PCA plot (Additional file 1: Fig. S3A), and in the Euclidean distance matrix which retains all pairwise relationships and quantifies the exact distance between samples, providing a clearer view of overall similarity and potential outliers (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>A). Only three genes were found to differ significantly (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05) between the sham and Adx spiny mice: <i>Thrsp</i> expression was reduced in the Adx group, and that of <i>Ikzf3</i> and <i>Bik</i> slightly increased (Additional file 2: Table 1, Additional file 1: Fig. 3B–D). However, the significance of this, or a role for these factors in ovarian function is unclear.</p><div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-5" data-title="Fig. 5"><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig5" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 5</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig5_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-5-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1186%2Fs12915-026-02619-2/MediaObjects/12915_2026_2619_Fig5_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 5" loading="lazy" width="685" height="365"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2/figures/5" data-track-dest="link:Figure5 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 5" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-5-desc"><p>DHEA administration resulted in an ovarian transcriptome with features characteristic of PCOS. <b>A</b> Distance matrix showing the samples from the three experimental groups used in the differential expression analysis. <b>B</b> GO analysis (in GOrilla) of the most enriched biological processes for the up- (log2FC &gt; 1) and down- (log2FC &gt; − 1) regulated genes (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05). <b>C</b>–<b>F</b> Box plots showing normalized counts of genes upregulated in AdxDHEA and involved with immune response: <b>C</b> <i>Il33</i>, <b>D</b> <i>Il18</i>, <b>E</b> <i>Tlr4</i>, and <b>F</b> Nfkb2. <b>G</b>–<b>M</b> Genes affected in the AdxDHEA group that are involved in E2-activated or activating pathways: <b>G</b> <i>Mgarp</i>, <b>H</b> <i>Fshr</i>, <b>I</b> <i>Cyp19a1</i>, <b>J</b> <i>Greb1</i>, <b>K</b> <i>Lhcgr</i>, <b>L</b> <i>Esr1</i>, <b>M</b> <i>Amhr2</i>. For all, sham (<i>n</i> = 6), Adx (<i>n</i> = 5), and AdxDHEA (<i>n</i> = 5); * <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05, ** <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.01, *** <i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.001, # <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05, otherwise non-significant (all analyzed in DESeq2). See also Fig. S3</p></div></div></figure></div><p>In contrast, the AdxDHEA group had 1453 DEGs relative to the Adx only group, with 329 downregulated and 1124 upregulated (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05; Additional file 4: Table 4). GO analysis revealed that the most upregulated genes (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 and log2FC &gt; 1) were enriched for biological function of the immune response (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>B); these included <i>Il33</i>, <i>Il18</i>, <i>Tlr4</i>, and <i>Nfkb2</i> (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>C–F). This was notable because these genes are elevated and promote immune responses in PCOS [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shamsi M, Ghazavi A, Saeedifar AM, Mosayebi G, Pour SK, Ganji A. The immune system’s role in PCOS. Mol Biol Rep. 2022;49:10689–702. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11033-022-07695-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR60" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2052">60</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Bansal B, Thazhuthadath Kishore A, Kathiresan S, Farook Ghachi A, Pradhan S, Paul S, et al. A systematic review of inflammatory markers in Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) and meta-analysis of interleukin-6 (IL-6) in case-control studies. Cureus. 2025;17. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.7759/CUREUS.82350&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR61" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2052_1">61</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 62" title="Rostamtabar M, Esmaeilzadeh S, Tourani M, Rahmani A, Baee M, Shirafkan F, et al. Pathophysiological roles of chronic low-grade inflammation mediators in polycystic ovary syndrome. J Cell Physiol. 2021;236:824–38. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1002/JCP.29912&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR62" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2055">62</a>].</p><p>The most downregulated genes (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 and log2FC &lt;-1) were enriched for the cholesterol synthesis pathway (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>B), which may also be characteristic of a PCOS-like condition [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 63" title="Salilew-Wondim D, Wang Q, Tesfaye D, Schellander K, Hoelker M, Hossain MM, et al. Polycystic ovarian syndrome is accompanied by repression of gene signatures associated with biosynthesis and metabolism of steroids, cholesterol and lipids. J Ovarian Res. 2015;8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/S13048-015-0151-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR63" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2067">63</a>]. These included genes encoding key enzymes like <i>Hmgcs1</i>, <i>Hmgcr</i>, <i>Fdft1</i>, <i>Cyp51</i>, and <i>Hsd17b7</i> (Fig. S3E–I) and indicate that the high levels of DHEA had some negative feedback on this pathway. Interesting also, Cyp51 was reported to induce oocyte meiotic resumption [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 64" title="Ning G, Ouyang H, Wang S, Chen X, Xu B, Yang J, et al. 3’,5’-cyclic adenosine monophosphate response element binding protein up-regulated cytochrome P450 lanosterol 14alpha-demethylase expression involved in follicle-stimulating hormone-induced mouse oocyte maturation. Mol Endocrinol. 2008;22:1682–94. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ME.2007-0480&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR64" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2086">64</a>].</p><p>Other downregulated genes included several known estrogen-responsive genes, such as <i>Mgarp</i>26, <i>Fshr</i>,<i> Cyp19a1</i>, and <i>Greb1</i> (the latter two significant before adjustment: Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>G–J) and <i>Lhcgr</i> (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>K), although the mRNA levels of the gene encoding the estrogen receptor ERα, <i>Esr1</i>, increased (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>L). Notably, the drop in mRNA levels of <i>Cyp19a1</i>, which encodes aromatase, indicates reduced capacity to convert androgens into estrogens, which was evident also in the relative circulating levels of T and E2 (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig4">4</a>G, H). Moreover, this decrease in <i>Cyp19a1</i> expression correlates also with the elevated mRNA levels of the anti-Müllerian hormone (Amh) receptor (<i>Amhr2</i>; Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#Fig5">5</a>M) given that AMH inhibits aromatase activity [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 65" title="Mullen RD, Ontiveros AE, Moses MM, Behringer RR. AMH and AMHR2 mutations: a spectrum of reproductive phenotypes across vertebrate species. Dev Biol. 2019;455:1–9. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.YDBIO.2019.07.006&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR65" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2137">65</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="Yamaguchi T, Kitano T. Amh/Amhr2 signaling causes masculinization by inhibiting estrogen synthesis during gonadal sex differentiation in Japanese flounder (Paralichthys olivaceus). Int J Mol Sci. 2023;24. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3390/IJMS24032480&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2140">66</a>], and increased expression of Amhr2 is a hallmark of PCOS [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 67" title="Catteau-Jonard S, Jamin SP, Leclerc A, Gonzalès J, Dewailly D, Di Clemente N. Anti-Mullerian hormone, its receptor, FSH receptor, and androgen receptor genes are overexpressed by granulosa cells from stimulated follicles in women with polycystic ovary syndrome. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2008;93:4456–61. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JC.2008-1231&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2143">67</a>]. By suppressing estrogen synthesis, increased <i>Amhr2</i> would contribute to the hormonal imbalance typical of the condition, characterized by a high androgen-to-estrogen ratio, clearly indicating a prepubertal PCOS-like phenotype in this model.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Discussion"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec8-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec8">Discussion</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec8-content"><p>Understanding the functional significance of adrenarche in human development has been hindered severely by the lack of an appropriate animal model [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Iñiguez G, Barnafi E, Lalli E, Pereira A, Ferrer P, Mericq V. Prolactin and adrenal androgens during adrenarche. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2025;103:66–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/CEN.15227&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2159">8</a>]. Commonly used laboratory rodents lack both endogenous prepubertal adrenal androgen production and the juvenile developmental phase, precluding mechanistic studies of how the childhood rise in DHEA(S) contributes to sexual maturation and reproductive programming. Our findings here establish the spiny mouse as a valuable model for dissecting the endocrine, molecular, and developmental consequences of early-life exposure to DHEA, as distinct from those of the more potent gonadal steroids produced at puberty.</p><p>The absence of an increase in hypothalamic and pituitary gonadotropic hormones during the spiny mice prepubertal rise in circulating DHEA, indicates that the reproductive axis has yet to be activated, paralleling the temporal separation of adrenarche and puberty in humans. Despite this, the ovarian transcriptome shows clear changes associated with maturation of the steroidogenic pathways at this time. Moreover, the Adx experiments confirm that DHEA can be produced in non-adrenal tissues, and the DHEA administered to these animals was metabolized to other steroids, though notably not to E2 or DHT. The likelihood that these reactions occur in the ovary is supported by expression of all of the required enzymes, as evident in the ovarian transcriptomes. Given that the spiny mouse clearly produces cortisol, which like DHEA requires Cyp17a1, an enzyme absent from the adrenal glands of most laboratory rodents, we do not dispute previous reports that the spiny mouse adrenal gland produces DHEA. Rather, our findings indicate that DHEA can also be synthesized, and even sulfonated, in other tissues. This is not entirely unexpected, given that steroidogenesis occurs in many tissues, though it is noteworthy that in these animals, some ovarian steroidogenesis appears to begin before gonadotropic activation at puberty. Although possibly species-specific, this observation has implications for understanding the potential role and regulation of peripheral androgen synthesis and suggests a more complex prepubertal hormone-signaling landscape than previously recognized.</p><p>Many of the changes in the ovarian transcriptome indicate estrogen-receptor signaling, including upregulation of Prlr and prolactin. Although some of these changes could be age-related rather than a direct consequence of the increase in DHEA, the cell culture experiments support a role for DHEA in activating these and other known estrogen-responsive genes via the estrogen receptor. A role for Prl in the prepubertal ovary is not established, and it is more typically associated with luteal function as it stimulates <i>Lhcgr</i> expression to mediate maintenance of the <i>corpus luteum</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Stocco C. The long and short of the prolactin receptor: the corpus luteum needs them both! Biol Reprod. 2012;86. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1095/BIOLREPROD.111.098293&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR68" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2174">68</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Binart N, Bachelot A, Bouilly J. Impact of prolactin receptor isoforms on reproduction. Trends Endocrinol Metab. 2010;21:362–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.TEM.2010.01.008&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR69" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2174_1">69</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="Bachelot A, Carré N, Mialon O, Matelot M, Servel N, Monget P, et al. The permissive role of prolactin as a regulator of luteinizing hormone action in the female mouse ovary and extragonadal tumorigenesis. Am J Physiol Endocrinol Metab. 2013;305. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1152/AJPENDO.00243.2013&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2177">70</a>]. Notably, however, Prl levels are tightly correlated to circulating adrenal androgen levels in children, its receptor is expressed in the human adrenal cortex and it has been proposed to play a role in adrenarche onset, including in the production of adrenal androgens [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Iñiguez G, Barnafi E, Lalli E, Pereira A, Ferrer P, Mericq V. Prolactin and adrenal androgens during adrenarche. Clin Endocrinol (Oxf). 2025;103:66–73. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1111/CEN.15227&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2180">8</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="Lalli E, Figueiredo BC. Prolactin as an adrenocorticotropic hormone: prolactin signalling is a conserved key regulator of sexually dimorphic adrenal gland function in health and disease. BioEssays. 2022;44:2200109." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2184">71</a>]. It is quite possible, therefore, that DHEA-induced prolactin signaling plays a role in upregulating expression of ovarian steroidogenic enzymes at the juvenile stage, priming ovarian function in preparation for puberty.</p><p>Beyond enabling assessment of the effects of the prepubertal endogenous rise in DHEA on the HPG axis, our findings also suggest the spiny as a suitable novel model for studying the well-known connection of high childhood DHEA/premature adrenarche with increased susceptibility to PCOS [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Livadas S, Bothou C, Kanaka-Gantenbein C, Chiotis D, Angelopoulos N, Macut D, et al. Unfavorable hormonal and psychologic profile in adult women with a history of premature adrenarche and pubarche, compared to women with polycystic ovary syndrome. Horm Metab Res. 2020;52:179–85. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1055/A-1109-2630&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2190">17</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ibañez L, Potau N, Virdis R, Zampolli M, Terzi C, Gussinyé M, et al. Postpubertal outcome in girls diagnosed of premature pubarche during childhood: increased frequency of functional ovarian hyperandrogenism. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 1993;76:1599–603. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JCEM.76.6.8501168&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2193">24</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ibáñez L, Díaz R, López-Bermejo A, Marcos MV. Clinical spectrum of premature pubarche: links to metabolic syndrome and ovarian hyperandrogenism. Rev Endocr Metab Disord. 2009;10:63–76. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11154-008-9096-Y&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR25" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2193_1">25</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 26" title="Stener-Victorin E, Padmanabhan V, Walters KA, Campbell RE, Benrick A, Giacobini P, et al. Animal models to understand the etiology and pathophysiology of polycystic ovary syndrome. Endocr Rev. 2020;41. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/ENDREV/BNAA010&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2196">26</a>]. Increasing DHEA levels during this developmental window was seen to augment androgen-to-estrogen ratios and induce gene expression patterns, many of which are typical of PCOS. <i>AMHR2</i>, elevated in both PCOS [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 67" title="Catteau-Jonard S, Jamin SP, Leclerc A, Gonzalès J, Dewailly D, Di Clemente N. Anti-Mullerian hormone, its receptor, FSH receptor, and androgen receptor genes are overexpressed by granulosa cells from stimulated follicles in women with polycystic ovary syndrome. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2008;93:4456–61. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JC.2008-1231&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2202">67</a>] and in the DHEA-treated spiny mice, encodes the receptor of the hormone AMH which is also increased in PCOS and induces the condition by inhibiting aromatase [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 67" title="Catteau-Jonard S, Jamin SP, Leclerc A, Gonzalès J, Dewailly D, Di Clemente N. Anti-Mullerian hormone, its receptor, FSH receptor, and androgen receptor genes are overexpressed by granulosa cells from stimulated follicles in women with polycystic ovary syndrome. J Clin Endocrinol Metab. 2008;93:4456–61. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/JC.2008-1231&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2206">67</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 72" title="Tata B, Mimouni NEH, Barbotin AL, Malone SA, Loyens A, Pigny P, et al. Elevated prenatal anti-Müllerian hormone reprograms the fetus and induces polycystic ovary syndrome in adulthood. Nat Med. 2018;24:834–46. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-018-0035-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR72" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2209">72</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 73" title="Butt MS, Saleem J, Aiman S, Zakar R, Sadique I, Fischer F. Serum anti-Müllerian hormone as a predictor of polycystic ovarian syndrome among women of reproductive age. BMC Womens Health. 2022;22. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/S12905-022-01782-2&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR73" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2212">73</a>]. Consistent with this mechanism, the expression of the aromatase-encoding <i>Cyp19a1</i>, was reduced following DHEA administration [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 74" title="Devillers MM, Petit F, Cluzet V, François CM, Giton F, Garrel G, et al. FSH inhibits AMH to support ovarian estradiol synthesis in infantile mice. J Endocrinol. 2019;240:215–28. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1530/JOE-18-0313&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR74" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2218">74</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 75" title="Grossman MP, Nakajima ST, Fallat ME, Siow Y. Müllerian-inhibiting substance inhibits cytochrome P450 aromatase activity in human granulosa lutein cell culture. Fertil Steril. 2008;89(5 Suppl):1364–70. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.FERTNSTERT.2007.03.066&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR75" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2221">75</a>], contributing to the typical PCOS hormonal imbalance [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="Garg D, Tal R. The role of AMH in the pathophysiology of polycystic ovarian syndrome. Reprod Biomed Online. 2016;33:15–28. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/J.RBMO.2016.04.007&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2225">76</a>]. Although estrogen levels are likely low prior to puberty, expression of the required steroidogenic enzymes in the ovary indicates that some might be produced, and a reduction in their levels could enhance immune activation and inflammation, consistent with the elevated expression of immune response-related genes [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 60" title="Shamsi M, Ghazavi A, Saeedifar AM, Mosayebi G, Pour SK, Ganji A. The immune system’s role in PCOS. Mol Biol Rep. 2022;49:10689–702. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/S11033-022-07695-5&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR60" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2228">60</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Luo M, Yang X, Zhou M, Zhang J, Yu B, Lian H, et al. Integrated single-cell and spatial transcriptomics reveal microenvironment disruptions by androgen in mouse ovary. iScience. 2024;27:111028. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.111028&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR77" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2231">77</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Wang J, Yin T, Liu S. Dysregulation of immune response in PCOS organ system. Front Immunol. 2023;14. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3389/FIMMU.2023.1169232&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR78" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2231_1">78</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 79" title="Sun B, Pan Z, Kuang H, Feng X, Hou L, Xu F, et al. TLR4/NF-κB signaling contributes to the inflammation in ovary and inguinal fats of polycystic ovary syndrome. bioRxiv. 2022;:2022.07.14.500077. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.07.14.500077&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR79" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2234">79</a>], which is also reminiscent of PCOS [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 80" title="Xu Y, Zhang Z, Wang R, Xue S, Ying Q, Jin L. Roles of estrogen and its receptors in polycystic ovary syndrome. Front Cell Dev Biol. 2024;12. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3389/FCELL.2024.1395331&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR80" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2237">80</a>].</p><p>Another physiological feature that suggests the spiny mouse might be a suitable model for studying PCOS, which we did not explore here, is its propensity to develop metabolic disease spontaneously under laboratory conditions. Unlike most lab rodents, spiny mice readily develop metabolic disturbances including glucose intolerance, and diabetes-like symptoms [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shafrir E, Adler JH. Enzymatic and metabolic responses to affluent diet of two diabetes-prone species of spiny mice: Acomys cahirinus and Acomys russatus. Int J Biochem. 1983;15:1439–46. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-711X(83)90076-9&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR81" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2244">81</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Gutzeit A, Renold AE, Cerasi E, Shafrir E. Effect of diet-induced obesity on glucose and insulin tolerance of a rodent with a low insulin response (Acomys cahirinus). Diabetes. 1979;28:777–84. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.2337/diab.28.8.777&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="#ref-CR82" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2244_1">82</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 83" title="Gonet AE, Stauffacher W, Pictet R, Renold AE. Obesity and diabetes mellitus with striking congenital hyperplasia of the islets of Langerhans in spiny mice (Acomys cahirinus) : I. Histological findings and preliminary metabolic observations. Diabetologia. 1966;1:162–71. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01257907&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR83" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2247">83</a>], that are central to PCOS pathology. Together, these characteristics of the spiny mouse demonstrate the parallels between early-life DHEA exposure and the later emergence of PCOS-like characteristics, as well as its effects on ovarian function in ways that predispose to long-term reproductive dysfunction.</p><p>While differences in steroid profiles and tissue sources between spiny mice and humans are possible and must be considered, including the relative abundance of 11-oxygenated adrenal androgens recently reported in children [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 54" title="Turcu AF, Rege J, Auchus RJ, Rainey WE. 11-Oxygenated androgens in health and disease. Nat Rev Endocrinol. 2020;16:284–96. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1038/S41574-020-0336-X&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR54" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2253">54</a>], the spiny mouse uniquely combines a childhood-like developmental stage with an endogenous prepubertal rise in DHEA. This makes it a powerful model for dissecting the developmental impact of early androgen exposure. Our findings demonstrate its capacity to reveal how prepubertal DHEA shapes the HPG axis and ovarian gene expression, providing a platform for advancing understanding of reproductive and metabolic programming during the lifespan.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Conclusions"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec9-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec9">Conclusions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec9-content"><p>Our findings position the spiny mouse as a valuable model for studying the developmental roles of early-life exposure to DHEA. The demonstration that DHEA can influence ovarian gene expression, receptor signaling, and steroid balance during a prepubertal window supports a model in which DHEA acts as an early modulator of reproductive tissue maturation and subsequent function. This work establishes a foundation for further future investigations into how disruptions in this endocrine axis contribute to disease risk, including PCOS, and highlights the broader utility of the spiny mouse for study of DHEA in health and disease.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Methods"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec10-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec10">Methods</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec10-content"><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec11">Spiny mice husbandry</h3><p>Spiny mice (<i>A. dimidiatus</i>, belonging to the <i>cahirinus-dimidiatus</i> complex, previously considered a <i>subspecies of A. cahirinus</i> [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 84" title="Hadid Y, Pavlíček T, Beiles A, Ianovici R, Raz S, Nevo E. Sympatric incipient speciation of spiny mice Acomys at “Evolution Canyon,” Israel. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2014;111:1043–8. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1073/PNAS.1322301111/-/DCSUPPLEMENTAL/PNAS.201322301SI.PDF&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR84" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2285">84</a>], and referred to as such in some studies [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 85" title="Riddell B, McDonough M, Ferguson A, Kimani JM, Gawriluk TR, Peng C, et al. Complex tissue regeneration in Lophuromys reveals a phylogenetic signal for enhanced regenerative ability in deomyine rodents. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2024;122:e2420726122. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1073/PNAS.2420726122&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR85" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2288">85</a>]) were held and handled humanely, after protocol approval, and in accordance with Technion and Tel-Aviv University IACUC guidelines. Female and male spiny mice kept in the zoological gardens at Tel-Aviv University (TAU) were paired in outside cages. They were fed once every 2 days with a mix of standard chow and vegetables, supplemented with sunflower seeds once a week. Spiny mice brought from TAU to the animal facility at the Technion were kept in groups of one or two males and one or two females in cages under controlled conditions of 12 h/12 h light–dark cycles and 25 °C. They were fed ad libitum with standard chow and water. The pups of these mice were used for the adrenalectomy experiment.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec12">Adrenalectomy and DHEA administration</h3><p>The animals were anesthetized in a chamber flushed with 3% isoflurane mixed with 100% O<sub>2</sub>, weighed and transferred to a heated pad at 37 °C. Anesthesia was via a nose cone delivering 2–2.5% isoflurane mixed with 100% O<sub>2</sub>. After shaving and disinfection (antiseptic iodine and 70% ethanol), a dorsal incision was made about 1 cm lateral to the spinal midline, exposing the adrenal gland which was gently scooped out using blunt tweezers. The muscle and skin were closed with absorbable and unabsorbable sutures, respectively, and the same operation performed on the other side. The spiny mice recovered under a heating lamp for 15 min. Buprenorphine (20 μg/kg) was given subcutaneously immediately after surgery and again 24 h later, under light anesthesia. Dexamethasone (water-soluble, Sigma D2915) was given once daily, at 200 μg/kg for the first 48 h and 100 μg/kg for the next 48 h. Drinking water was replaced with saline solution and 1 ml Ringer solution was injected starting 3 days post-surgery until sacrifice, to overcome aldosterone depletion. Sham mice were operated similarly and adrenal glands were exposed before immediate closure. They were treated by analgesia as described, without dexamethasone, saline or Ringer solution.</p><p>Peanut butter (PB) was used to administer DHEA, prepared according to a published method [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 86" title="Ström JO, Theodorsson A, Ingberg E, Isaksson IM, Theodorsson E. Ovariectomy and 17β-estradiol replacement in rats and mice: a visual demonstration. J Vis Exp. 2012. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.3791/4013&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR86" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2306">86</a>]. The DHEA (Sigma) was added to corn oil to reach final concentration of 24 μg/μl, and mixed with natural 100% PB to a final concentration of 16 μg DHEA/1.22 μl PB. Spiny mice were given 3.66 μl PB/g BW daily. This dose (48 µg DHEA/g BW/d) was chosen to achieve circulating levels comparable to those observed in 23–24 d old spiny mice, based on published DHEA measurements after its ingestion [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 87" title="Jimenez PT, Frolova AI, Chi MM, Grindler NM, Willcockson AR, Reynolds KA, et al. DHEA-mediated inhibition of the pentose phosphate pathway alters oocyte lipid metabolism in mice. Endocrinology. 2013;154:4835–44. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1210/en.2012-2140&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR87" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2309">87</a>] and a small pilot study. During administration, each spiny mouse was separated into a different cage for exact dosing. After adrenalectomy, they were assigned randomly into treatment groups; sham-operated spiny mice received vehicle-only. At 7 days post-surgery, they were euthanized 4 h after PB administration, and serum and HPG tissues harvested for steroid and RNA measurement.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec13">Serum sampling and steroid measurement</h3><p>Pups were euthanized by isoflurane overdose and blood collected by cardiac puncture. Whole blood was incubated for 20 min at room temperature (RT), then centrifuged for 10 min, 2000 g at 4 °C; serum was collected and stored at − 80 °C. DHEA concentration was measured in 1:5 diluted serum by ELISA (Enzo Life Sciences, ADI-900—093), following the manufacturer’s protocol. Alternatively, steroid levels were measured by LC–MS at the Biomedical Core Facilities at the Rappaport Faculty of Medicine, Technion-Israel Institute of Technology. Samples (100 μl) were subjected to liquid–liquid extraction (LLE) using 3 ml of tert-butyl methyl ether (MTBE), frozen to facilitate phase separation, and the organic layer was decanted and evaporated at 37 °C under a stream of nitrogen. The dried extracts were reconstituted in 100 μl of 50% methanol, and a 10-μl aliquot was injected into a Vanquish Flex UHPLC system (Thermo Scientific). Chromatographic separation was using a Thermo Scientific™ Accucore™ AQ reversed-phase column (2.6 μm, 100 × 2.1 mm) maintained at 40 °C. Each sample was analyzed in both positive and negative ionization modes to maximize steroid coverage. Mass spectrometric analysis was performed on an Orbitrap Exploris™ 240 mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) equipped with a heated electrospray ionization (HESI) source. Spray voltage was set to 3500 V (positive mode) and 3250 V (negative mode); sheath and auxiliary gases were set to 53 and 14 arbitrary units, respectively. The ion transfer tube and vaporizer temperatures were set to 269 °C and 450 °C, respectively. Full MS scans were acquired at a resolution of 120,000 (at m/z 200) over a scan range of m/z 100–1000. The RF lens was set to 70%, and all steroid ions were detected with a mass accuracy within 5 ppm. Data acquisition was performed using Xcalibur software (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Data analysis was using Skyline Daily [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 88" title="Adams KJ, Pratt B, Bose N, Dubois LG, St. John-Williams L, Perrott KM, et al. Skyline for small molecules: a unifying software package for quantitative metabolomics. J Proteome Res. 2020;19:1447–58. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1021/ACS.JPROTEOME.9B00640&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR88" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2320">88</a>] with the instrument set to Orbitrap and a resolution of 120,000 (at m/z 200). Extracted ion chromatograms (XICs) were generated based on a transition list containing compound names, accurate masses, explicit retention times (± 1 min), polarity, and main adducts. Chromatographic peaks were integrated and used for relative quantification based on peak area.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec14">RNA extraction, reverse transcription and quantitative PCR (qPCR)</h3><p>Tissues were collected into TRIzol Reagent (Life Technologies), homogenized and kept at −80 °C until RNA extraction. The RNA fractions were isolated from each sample by phase separation, and RNA extracted from the upper phase with RNA Clean &amp; Concentrator kit with DNase treatment (Zymo R1014) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. RNA concentrations were determined (for RNA from tissues or primary cells) using Qubit Fluorometer kits (broad range or high sensitivity, Invitrogen), according to the manufacturer’s protocol, or (for RNA from cell lines) by Nanodrop, and RNA integrity measured using Agilent 2200 TapeStation. Reverse transcription was performed using Quanta-bio qScript cDNA synthesis kit (Quantabio 95,047—100) according to the manufacturer’s instructions, with 0.1–1 μg of RNA. Quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR) was carried out with qPCRBIO SyGreen blue mix (PB20, PCR Biosystems), on 0.1–1 ng of cDNA template. The amplicon levels were analyzed in triplicate and quantified using standard curves, before normalizing to levels of <i>Actb</i> or <i>Rplp0</i> in spiny mice samples, as indicated. All primers used for qPCR amplification are given in Supplementary Table S5.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec15">Long-read RNA sequencing (RNA-seq)</h3><p>Long-read transcriptome sequencing was adopted initially to provide accurate read mapping in the context of a novel animal model. Library preparation, sequencing and initial analysis of hypothalamic and ovarian RNA from 7 to 8 days and 23 to 24 days spiny mice were performed by LoopGenomics using LoopSeq™ Transcriptome 3 × 8-plex Kit. This method selectively reverse transcribes polyA-tailed RNA and barcodes each molecule with a unique molecular identifier (UMI). The samples were sequenced on a NextSeq 550 Illumina system using 2 × 150 bp paired-end reads. The reference genome and annotation used for alignment and mapping were provided by EMBL-EBI (version mAcoCah1_scaffolds_1811). Differential expression analysis was done using “Deseq2” package in R [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 89" title="Love MI, Huber W, Anders S. Moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion for RNA-seq data with DESeq2. Genome Biol. 2014;15:1–21. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-014-0550-8&#xA;                  &#xA;                . " href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR89" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2346">89</a>]. Gene ontology of the hypothalami was conducted using “GOrilla” [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 90" title="Eden E, Navon R, Steinfeld I, Lipson D, Yakhini Z. GOrilla: a tool for discovery and visualization of enriched GO terms in ranked gene lists. BMC Bioinformatics. 2009;10:1–7. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-10-48&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR90" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2349">90</a>] with one ranked list of either all upregulated or downregulated genes, and for the ovaries using a target list of either upregulated (log2FC &gt; 1) or downregulated (log2FC &lt; -1) genes compared with a background list of all genes.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec16">Transcription factor (TF) motif analysis</h3><p>The promoters and 5′ regions of the DEGs (<i>P</i>. adj &lt; 0.05 in ovaries and <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05 in hypothalami) were defined as 1000 bp upstream to the TSS, and 100 bp downstream. These sequences were extracted and examined for enriched motifs and their associated transcription factors (TFs), using the XSTREME tool in the MEME suite.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec17">RNA-seq ovary transcriptome analysis</h3><p>The RNA of ovarian samples from the adrenalectomy experiment were extracted and RNA prepared with NEBNext Ultra-Express RNA library prep and sequenced by NextSeq 2000 (P2 XLEAP, 100 cycles single-read) at the Technion Genome Center. The reads were mapped and annotated to the published spiny mouse genome assembly [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 91" title="Nguyen ED, Fard VN, Kim BY, Collins S, Galey M, Nelson BR, et al. Genome report: chromosome-scale genome assembly of the African spiny mouse (Acomys cahirinus). G3 (Bethesda). 2023;13. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1093/G3JOURNAL/JKAD177&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR91" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2374">91</a>]. Samples from all three groups were subjected to Principle component analysis (PCA) and Euclidean distance matrix done with “Deseq2” and “pheatmap” packages in R, respectively. Pairwise comparisons for differential expression were then performed separately for the sham vs Adx and Adx vs Adx + DHEA samples, using the “Deseq2” package in R.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec18">Cell line and primary ovarian cultures</h3><p>GH3 cells were maintained in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 1% pen-str, 1% sodium pyruvate (Biological Industries). Prior to treatments, cells were starved for 24 h using medium containing charcoal-stripped FBS and treatments were in the same media, with or without 17β-estradiol (E2) or DHEA. KK-1 murine granulosa cells were cultured in DMEM/F-12 medium supplemented with 10% FBS and 1% pen-strep and treated similarly with E2, DHEA or DHT, as noted. All cell lines were kept in a humidified incubator with 5% CO2 at 37 °C and were sub-cultured at 80–90% confluency.</p><p>Spiny mice ovaries were collected into cold HBSS and transferred to a papain solution (1 mg/ml) for 20 min at 37 °C. They were then transferred to fresh medium DMEM/F12 containing 10% charcoal-stripped FBS (both Gibco), 1% penicillin–streptomycin (Pen-str) and further dissociated by cell strainer. Cells from each animal were cultured in 4 wells of a 48-well plate for 24 h, and then left untreated (NT) or exposed to DHEA as noted for 48 h.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec19">Chromatin immunoprecipitation</h3><p>ChIP for ERα was performed essentially as previously [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 92" title="Luo M, Koh M, Feng J, Wu Q, Melamed P. Cross talk in hormonally regulated gene transcription through induction of estrogen receptor ubiquitylation. Mol Cell Biol. 2005;25:7386–98. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1128/mcb.25.16.7386-7398.2005&#xA;                  &#xA;                ." href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR92" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2396">92</a>]. GH3 cells were cross-linked with 1% formaldehyde for 10 min, before quenching with glycine (125 mM final concentration) for 5 min. The cells were washed twice and collected into cold PBS. After centrifugation (1000 × g for 5 min), they were lysed with 750 μl lysis buffer, sonicated 10 times using a Sonics Vibra-Cell sonicator while submerged in ice water, to generate ~ 500 bp fragments. The cell debris was pelleted (10,000 × g for 5 min), and 50 μl of supernatant was removed to serve as the input. The remainder was immunoprecipitated by adding RIPA buffer, primary antibody against ERα (5 μg; Abcam ab32063, and protein G magnetic beads (20 μl; A63880 AMPure XP) with overnight incubation on a roller at 4 °C. After three washes with RIPA buffer, the samples were eluted (1% SDS, 100 mM NaHCO3). The cross-linking in the immunoprecipitated and input samples was reversed by incubation at 65 °C for 5 h, with addition of 2 μl RNase A (0.5 mg/ml), before purification using the PCR purification kit (28,106; Qiagen). Real-time qPCR was used to measure the levels of DNA in the immunoprecipitated and input samples. Primers are in Supplementary Table S5.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="Sec20">Statistical analysis</h3><p>All data are from multiple biological repeats (<i>n</i>-value, as stated in figure legends) which were assayed individually. <i>F</i>-test determined similarity of variance and data was analyzed accordingly by Student’s <i>t</i> test (two-tailed), or ANOVA followed by Tukey HSD post hoc test for multiple comparisons, to establish significance which was defined as <i>P</i> value &lt; 0.05. Graphs show mean ± SE, and for box plots, whiskers represent minimum and maximum values, boxes are 25–75% data ranges, and horizontal lines within boxes indicate the median values. All differential expression analyses performed on high-throughput data were done by “Deseq2” package [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 89" title="Love MI, Huber W, Anders S. Moderated estimation of fold change and dispersion for RNA-seq data with DESeq2. Genome Biol. 2014;15:1–21. &#xA;                  https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-014-0550-8&#xA;                  &#xA;                . " href="/article/10.1186/s12915-026-02619-2#ref-CR89" id="ref-link-section-d376777042e2419">89</a>] in R, which is based on Wald test and FDR adjustment for multiple comparisons calculated by the Benjamini–Hochberg method. For these transcriptome results, significant Padj values (<i>P</i> .adj &lt; 0.05) are marked with asterisks (*), and those significant before adjustment (<i>P</i> &lt; 0.05) are marked similarly with ladder symbols (#), as noted in the figure legends.</p></div></div></section>
                                </div>
                        
                    

                        

                    
                        
                    

                    

                    <section data-title="Data availability"><div class="c-article-section" id="data-availability-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="data-availability">Data availability</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="data-availability-content">
              
              <p>Data are available in the manuscript and additional files, and at NCBI’s Gene Expression Omnibus (GSE306393).
</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="Abbreviations"><div class="c-article-section" id="abbreviations-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="abbreviations">Abbreviations</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="abbreviations-content"><dl class="c-abbreviation_list"><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>DHEA:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Dehydroepiandrosterone</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>DHEAS:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>DHEA(S):</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Dehydroepiandrosterone and its sulfated form</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>HPG axis:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>PCOS:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Polycystic ovary syndrome</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>E2:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Estradiol</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>T:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Testosterone</p>
                  </dd><dt class="c-abbreviation_list__term u-text-bold u-float-left u-pr-16" style="min-width:50px;"><dfn>DHT:</dfn></dt><dd class="c-abbreviation_list__description u-mb-24">
                    <p>Dihydrotestosterone</p>
